IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cra-dates`

-- Compensation to Relatives Act 1897 (NSW) -- the whole operative Act (ss 3-8), from the seat of a relative of a person
-- whose death was wrongfully caused.
--
-- SEAT: the relative (spouse, de facto partner, brother, sister, half-brother, half-sister, parent or child of the dead
-- person, as s 7 widens "parent" and "child") for whose benefit the action is brought. Every exported question is one
-- that person would ask: "Is someone liable for my relative's death?", "Can I claim?", "Can I bring the action myself?",
-- "From what day?", "What must I give the defendant?", "Are the funeral and headstone costs recoverable?", "Will the
-- insurance or super I received be taken off my damages?", "What happens to a payment into court?", "How much do I
-- receive?". The court, the jury and the executor get no seat: what they do is encoded as what it means for the relative.
--
-- Provisions: s 3 (the cause of action, funeral and headstone costs, sums not taken into account); s 4 (who brings the
-- action and for whom; division of the amount recovered); s 5 (one action only); s 6 (particulars); s 6A (payment into
-- court); s 6B (a relative's own action); s 6C (survival against the wrongdoer's estate); s 6D (trial by judge alone);
-- s 6E (application); s 7 (definitions); s 8 (the 1999 amendments and past events). Out: s 1 (name), s 2 and the
-- Schedule (repeal): machinery. The time limit for the action is not in this Act (it is in the Limitation Act 1969,
-- s 19, a separate subject).
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no (a "means" definition, a
-- condition introduced by "if", "where" or "whensoever", a flat "shall not"), or NOTHING where the Act does not answer on
-- the facts given; each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind" comment.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; version as at 15 June 2018; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for
-- public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Compensation to Relatives Act 1897`

§§ `The nouns`

-- The relative asking, by their relationship to the dead person ("the person whose death has been so caused").
-- s 4(1) lists "the spouse, brother, sister, half-brother, half-sister, parent, and child"; s 7(1) says "parent shall
-- include father and mother, and grandfather and grandmother, and stepfather and stepmother, and any person standing in
-- loco parentis to another" and "child shall include son and daughter, and grandson and granddaughter, and stepson and
-- stepdaughter, and any person to whom another stands in loco parentis"; s 7(4) "spouse means (a) the person to whom a
-- person is legally married ... or (b) a de facto partner" (de facto partner is defined in s 21C of the Interpretation
-- Act 1987, a borrowed term asked as a fact).
DECLARE `My relationship to the person who died` IS ONE OF
    `I was legally married to them`
    `I was their de facto partner`
    `I am their brother or sister`
    `I am their half-brother or half-sister`
    `I am their father or mother`
    `I am their grandfather or grandmother`
    `I am their stepfather or stepmother`
    `I stood in loco parentis to them`
    `I am their son or daughter`
    `I am their grandson or granddaughter`
    `I am their stepson or stepdaughter`
    `they stood in loco parentis to me`
    `none of these`

-- The costs s 3(2) names, and anything else.
DECLARE `The cost I am asking about` IS ONE OF
    `the funeral`
    `the cremation`
    `erecting a headstone or tombstone over the grave`
    `some other cost of the death`

-- The sums s 3(3) names, and anything else.
DECLARE `The money I received because of the death` IS ONE OF
    `a sum paid or payable on the death under a contract of insurance`
    `a sum paid or payable out of a superannuation, provident or like fund`
    `a benefit from a friendly society, benefit society or trade union`
    `a pension under one of the listed pension Acts, or an Act amending or replacing one`
    `some other money`

-- The answer to "what does section 6 mean for me?"
DECLARE `What section 6 means for me` IS ONE OF
    `I am not the plaintiff on the record, so section 6 puts no duty on me`
    `I delivered the particulars together with the statement of claim, as section 6 requires`
    `I have not delivered the particulars section 6 requires together with the statement of claim`

-- The parties and acts of the two regulative rules.
DECLARE `Claim party` IS ONE OF `the relative`, `the plaintiff on the record`
DECLARE `Claim act` IS ONE OF
    `bring the action`
    `deliver the full particulars to the defendant`

§§ `s 3(1) -- the cause of action`

-- s 3(1) "Whensoever the death of a person is caused by a wrongful act, neglect, or default, and the act, neglect, or
-- default is such as would (if death had not ensued) have entitled the party injured to maintain an action and recover
-- damages in respect thereof, then ... the person who would have been liable if death had not ensued shall be liable to an
-- action for damages, notwithstanding the death of the person injured, and although the death has been caused under such
-- circumstances as amount in law to a serious indictable offence." "Whensoever" is a "when": a failed condition is
-- JUST FALSE. Whether the dead person could have sued is the general law of torts (a borrowed question, asked).
-- s 6C(1) "Every action and cause of action under this Act shall survive notwithstanding the death of the wrongdoer."
-- s 6C(3): damages recovered against the wrongdoer's executor or administrator are payable in like order of
-- administration as the wrongdoer's debts. s 6E(1), (3): the Act applies wherever the matter arose and whatever anyone's
-- nationality, and binds the Crown; none of these facts changes the answer, so they are not asked (fork F7).
@export Is the person who caused my relative's death liable to an action for damages under the Act (ss 3(1), 6C(1))?
GIVEN `the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your relative's death was caused by someone's wrongful act, neglect or default (for example negligent driving, an unsafe workplace, medical negligence)
      `my relative could have sued for it had they lived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that act, neglect or default would have entitled your relative, had they not died, to sue and recover damages for it (the general law, for example negligence, decides this)
      `the death amounted in law to a serious indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused in circumstances amounting in law to a serious indictable offence (for example manslaughter or dangerous driving causing death)
      `the person responsible has since died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who would have been liable (the wrongdoer) has since died
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is someone liable for my relative's death` MEANS
    IF     `the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default`
       AND `my relative could have sued for it had they lived`
    THEN JUST TRUE  -- "although" a serious indictable offence (s 3(1)); "notwithstanding the death of the wrongdoer" (s 6C(1))
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `ss 4, 7, 8 -- for whom the action is brought`

-- s 4(1) "Every such action shall be for the benefit of the spouse, brother, sister, half-brother, half-sister, parent,
-- and child of the person whose death has been so caused". s 4(2): where there is more than one spouse, the action is for
-- the benefit of each, as separate parties. s 8 "The amendments made to this Act by the Property (Relationships)
-- Legislation Amendment Act 1999 do not operate to confer on any person a right of action in relation to any act,
-- neglect or default that took place before those amendments took effect." Those amendments are read as the ones that
-- brought de facto partners in (fork F2). The s 4(1) list is not closed with "only": a relationship outside it is
-- NOTHING (fork F1).
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the person who died`
      `the wrongful act happened before the 1999 amendments took effect` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the action is for the benefit of` `my relationship` `the wrongful act happened before the 1999 amendments took effect` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my relationship`
    WHEN `I was their de facto partner` THEN
        IF `the wrongful act happened before the 1999 amendments took effect`
        THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 8, a flat "do not operate to confer" (fork F2)
        ELSE JUST TRUE
    WHEN `none of these` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the s 4(1) list is not closed with "only"; fork F1)
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@export Can I claim compensation for my relative's death: is the action for my benefit (ss 3(1), 4, 7, 8)?
GIVEN `my relationship to the person who died` IS A `My relationship to the person who died` @desc Your relationship to the person who died. One of: "I was legally married to them", "I was their de facto partner", "I am their brother or sister", "I am their half-brother or half-sister", "I am their father or mother", "I am their grandfather or grandmother", "I am their stepfather or stepmother", "I stood in loco parentis to them" (in the place of a parent), "I am their son or daughter", "I am their grandson or granddaughter", "I am their stepson or stepdaughter", "they stood in loco parentis to me", "none of these"
      `the wrongful act happened before the 1999 amendments took effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act, neglect or default that caused the death took place before the amendments made by the Property (Relationships) Legislation Amendment Act 1999 took effect (this matters only to a de facto partner)
      `the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your relative's death was caused by someone's wrongful act, neglect or default
      `my relative could have sued for it had they lived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that act, neglect or default would have entitled your relative, had they not died, to sue and recover damages for it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I claim for my relative's death` MEANS
    IF `Is someone liable for my relative's death` `the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default` `my relative could have sued for it had they lived` FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE `the action is for the benefit of` `my relationship to the person who died` `the wrongful act happened before the 1999 amendments took effect`

§§ `ss 4(1), 5, 6B -- who brings the action`

-- s 4(1) the action "shall be brought by and in the name of the executor or administrator of the person deceased".
-- s 6B(1) "Where there is no executor or administrator of the person deceased, or where the person's executor or
-- administrator does not bring an action under this Act within six months after the death of the person deceased, the
-- person or any one or more of the persons for whose benefit the action might be brought ... may bring the action."
-- Written as a rule: while the executor or administrator still has the six months, a relative shall not bring the
-- action; when the six months pass unused, the bar lifts (HENCE FULFILLED). Time 0 is the day of death, in days.
-- (Jurally the relative has no power, rather than a duty; the rule models the absent power as SHANT.)
GIVEN `the days in the six months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim act`
`ss 4(1), 6B(1) -- while the executor or administrator has six months, the relative shall not bring the action` `the days in the six months` MEANS
    PARTY `the relative`
    SHANT `bring the action`
    WITHIN `the days in the six months`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the relative` BECAUSE "a relative brought the action within six months after the death while there was an executor or administrator who had not brought it (ss 4(1), 6B(1))"

-- The last day of "six months after the death" (fork F5).
GIVEN `the day of death` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of the six months after` `the day of death` MEANS `the day of death` `plus months` 6

@export If my relative's executor or administrator does not bring the action, from what day may I bring it myself (s 6B(1))?
GIVEN `the day my relative died` IS A DATE @desc The day your relative died
GIVETH A DATE
`From what day may I bring the action myself` MEANS
    (`the last day of the six months after` `the day my relative died`) `plus days` 1

-- s 5 "Not more than one action shall lie for and in respect of the same subject matter of complaint." A flat negative.
-- s 7(2), (3): administrator (under the Probate and Administration Act 1898, including the NSW Trustee and Guardian
-- collecting under an order to collect) and executor (to whom probate has been granted, including by representation).
@export Can I bring the action for my relative's death myself, in my own name, on the day I plan to (ss 4(1), 5, 6B)?
GIVEN `my relationship to the person who died` IS A `My relationship to the person who died` @desc Your relationship to the person who died. One of: "I was legally married to them", "I was their de facto partner", "I am their brother or sister", "I am their half-brother or half-sister", "I am their father or mother", "I am their grandfather or grandmother", "I am their stepfather or stepmother", "I stood in loco parentis to them", "I am their son or daughter", "I am their grandson or granddaughter", "I am their stepson or stepdaughter", "they stood in loco parentis to me", "none of these"
      `the wrongful act happened before the 1999 amendments took effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act, neglect or default that caused the death took place before the amendments made by the Property (Relationships) Legislation Amendment Act 1999 took effect (this matters only to a de facto partner)
      `there is an executor or administrator` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your relative has an executor (a person granted probate of the will) or an administrator (appointed under the Probate and Administration Act 1898, including the NSW Trustee and Guardian collecting the estate under an order to collect)
      `an action under the Act has already been brought for the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an action under this Act for the same death has already been brought, by the executor, the administrator or any relative
      `the day my relative died` IS A DATE @desc The day your relative died
      `the day I plan to bring the action` IS A DATE @desc The day you would start the court action
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I bring the action myself` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `my benefit` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `an action under the Act has already been brought for the death` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT `there is an executor or administrator`) THEN `my benefit`
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`ss 4(1), 6B(1) -- while the executor or administrator has six months, the relative shall not bring the action` `the six months`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the relative` `bring the action` `the day I bring it`))
               WHEN FULFILLED THEN `my benefit`
               OTHERWISE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `my benefit` MEANS `the action is for the benefit of` `my relationship to the person who died` `the wrongful act happened before the 1999 amendments took effect`
        `the six months` MEANS `the days from` `the day my relative died` `to` (`the last day of the six months after` `the day my relative died`)
        `the day I bring it` MEANS `the days from` `the day my relative died` `to` `the day I plan to bring the action`

§§ `s 6 -- the particulars the plaintiff must deliver`

-- s 6 "In every such action the plaintiff on the record shall be required, together with the declaration, to deliver to
-- the defendant or the defendant's attorney a full particular of the persons for whom and on whose behalf such action is
-- brought, and of the nature of the claim in respect of which damages are sought to be recovered." s 7(1) "declaration
-- shall include any statement of the cause of action appropriate to the court": today, the statement of claim. Time 0 is
-- the day the statement of claim is delivered; "together with" is WITHIN 0 (the same day). Particulars delivered earlier
-- are counted as delivered with it (fork F6).
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim act`
`s 6 -- the plaintiff must deliver full particulars together with the declaration` MEANS
    PARTY `the plaintiff on the record`
    MUST `deliver the full particulars to the defendant`
    WITHIN 0
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the plaintiff on the record` BECAUSE "full particulars of the persons for whom the action is brought, and of the nature of the claim, not delivered together with the declaration (s 6)"

@export If I am the plaintiff in the action, have I delivered to the defendant the particulars section 6 requires, together with my statement of claim (ss 6, 6B(2), 7(1))?
GIVEN `I am the plaintiff on the record` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the action is brought in your name (for example because you brought it yourself under s 6B, or you are the executor or administrator)
      `the day I delivered the statement of claim` IS A DATE @desc The day you delivered your statement of claim (the "declaration") to the defendant
      `I delivered particulars to the defendant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you delivered particulars of the claim to the defendant or the defendant's lawyer
      `the day I delivered the particulars` IS A DATE @desc The day you delivered those particulars (any day if you have not)
      `the particulars name every person the action is for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the particulars name every person for whom and on whose behalf the action is brought
      `the particulars state the nature of the claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the particulars state the nature of the claim for which damages are sought
GIVETH A `What section 6 means for me`
`Have I delivered the section 6 particulars` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I am the plaintiff on the record`)
    THEN `I am not the plaintiff on the record, so section 6 puts no duty on me`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 6 -- the plaintiff must deliver full particulars together with the declaration` 0 `the events`
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `I delivered the particulars together with the statement of claim, as section 6 requires`
         OTHERWISE `I have not delivered the particulars section 6 requires together with the statement of claim`
    WHERE
        `full particulars` MEANS `I delivered particulars to the defendant` AND `the particulars name every person the action is for` AND `the particulars state the nature of the claim`
        `the delay` MEANS `the days from` `the day I delivered the statement of claim` `to` `the day I delivered the particulars`
        `the time delivered` MEANS IF `the delay` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the delay`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `full particulars`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the plaintiff on the record` `deliver the full particulars to the defendant` `the time delivered`)
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL 1)

§§ `s 3(2), (3) -- what is recovered, and what is not taken into account`

-- s 3(2) "In any such action any reasonable expenses of the funeral or the cremation of the deceased person and the
-- reasonable cost of erecting a headstone or tombstone over the grave of the deceased person may be recovered."
-- Reasonableness is the court's judgment, asked. A grant limited by its own words ("reasonable"); a cost outside it,
-- or another kind of cost, is NOTHING: the Act does not say it cannot be recovered as damages.
@export In the action for my relative's death, can this funeral, cremation or headstone cost be recovered (s 3(2))?
GIVEN `the cost` IS A `The cost I am asking about` @desc The cost you are asking about. One of: "the funeral", "the cremation", "erecting a headstone or tombstone over the grave", "some other cost of the death"
      `the court finds it reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the expense (or cost) reasonable
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can this funeral or headstone cost be recovered` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the cost`
    WHEN `some other cost of the death` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent on other costs)
    OTHERWISE
        IF `the court finds it reasonable`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited by its own wording, "reasonable"; the case falls outside the limit)

-- s 3(3) "In assessing damages in any such action there shall not be taken into account: (a) any sum paid or payable on
-- the death of the deceased under any contract of insurance, or (b) any sum paid or payable out of any superannuation,
-- provident, or like fund, or by way of benefit from a friendly society, benefit society, or trade union, or (c) any sum
-- paid or payable by way of pension under: [five Acts of 1908-1945] ... or under any Act (Commonwealth or State)
-- amending or replacing any such Act." A flat "shall not": JUST FALSE. Whether a pension Act "replaces" a listed one
-- is asked (fork F4). Other money is left to the general law of damages: NOTHING.
@export Will this money I received because of my relative's death be taken into account (deducted) when my damages are assessed (s 3(3))?
GIVEN `the money` IS A `The money I received because of the death` @desc The money you are asking about. One of: "a sum paid or payable on the death under a contract of insurance", "a sum paid or payable out of a superannuation, provident or like fund", "a benefit from a friendly society, benefit society or trade union", "a pension under one of the listed pension Acts, or an Act amending or replacing one" (the Widows' Pension Act 1925 (NSW), the Coal and Oil Shale Mine Workers (Pensions) Act 1941 (NSW), and the Commonwealth Australian Soldiers' Repatriation Act 1920, Widows' Pensions Act 1942 and Invalid and Old-age Pensions Act 1908, or an Act amending or replacing one of them), "some other money"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Will this money be taken into account` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the money`
    WHEN `some other money` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent; the general law of damages decides)
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 6A -- payment into court`

-- s 6A(1) "the defendant may pay money into court as a compensation in one sum to all persons entitled under this Act
-- ... without specifying the shares". s 6A(2) "If the sum paid in is not accepted, and if an issue is taken by the
-- plaintiff as to its sufficiency, and the jury think the same sufficient, the defendant shall be entitled to the verdict
-- upon that issue." Conditions introduced by "if": a failed one is JUST FALSE. s 6D: a judge sitting without a jury
-- stands for the jury.
@export If the defendant pays money into court and we do not accept it but dispute whether it is enough, does the defendant win that issue (ss 6A(2), 6D)?
GIVEN `the defendant paid money into court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant paid money into court as compensation to all the relatives in one sum
      `the sum paid in was accepted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the sum paid in was accepted
      `the plaintiff took issue as to its sufficiency` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the plaintiff disputed that the sum paid in is enough
      `the jury think it sufficient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the jury (or the judge, if the action is tried without a jury) thinks the sum paid in is enough
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the defendant win the issue of the payment into court` MEANS
    IF     `the defendant paid money into court`
       AND (NOT `the sum paid in was accepted`)
       AND `the plaintiff took issue as to its sufficiency`
       AND `the jury think it sufficient`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 6A(3) "No portion of the sum paid in shall be paid out of court except under the order of a judge." A flat "shall
-- not ... except": without the order, JUST FALSE; with it, JUST TRUE as far as the order goes (fork F3).
@export Can any of the money the defendant paid into court be paid out to me (s 6A(3))?
GIVEN `a judge has ordered it paid out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a judge has ordered that money paid into court be paid out
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can money paid into court be paid out to me` MEANS
    IF `a judge has ordered it paid out` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 4(1) -- dividing what is recovered`

-- s 4(1) "the jury may give such damages as they may think proportioned to the injury resulting from such death to the
-- parties respectively ... and the amount so recovered, after deducting the costs not recovered from the defendant, shall
-- be divided amongst the before-mentioned parties in such shares as the jury by their verdict find and direct." s 6D:
-- the judge stands for the jury when there is none. The Act says nothing where the unrecovered costs exceed the amount
-- recovered: NOTHING.
@export How much of the amount recovered is left to divide among the relatives after the costs not recovered from the defendant (s 4(1))?
GIVEN `the amount recovered` IS A NUMBER @desc The total amount of damages recovered in the action, in dollars
      `the costs not recovered from the defendant` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs of the action that were not recovered from the defendant, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much is left to divide among the relatives` MEANS
    IF `the costs not recovered from the defendant` GREATER THAN `the amount recovered`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent where the costs exceed the amount recovered)
    ELSE JUST (`the amount recovered` MINUS `the costs not recovered from the defendant`)

@export How much do I receive from the amount recovered, given the share the jury (or judge) directs for me (ss 4(1), 6D)?
GIVEN `the amount recovered` IS A NUMBER @desc The total amount of damages recovered in the action, in dollars
      `the costs not recovered from the defendant` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs of the action that were not recovered from the defendant, in dollars
      `my share as a percentage` IS A NUMBER @desc The share the jury's verdict (or the judge's judgment, if tried without a jury) directs for you, as a percentage of the amount to be divided (for example 25 for a quarter)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much do I receive` MEANS
    CONSIDER `How much is left to divide among the relatives` `the amount recovered` `the costs not recovered from the defendant`
    WHEN JUST `the amount to divide` THEN
        IF `my share as a percentage` LESS THAN 0 OR `my share as a percentage` GREATER THAN 100
        THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (no such share can be directed)
        ELSE JUST ((`the amount to divide` TIMES `my share as a percentage`) DIVIDED BY 100)
    OTHERWISE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent where the costs exceed the amount recovered)
